[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one thing: the parser reads dates month first, 412 reports,
one fix queued. That is true of some of the reports. It is not true of all of them.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is not one issue. It is three, merged because [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)
merges on subject line alone, and all three read the same to a tired desk at 9am.
</Callout>

## What the table already shows

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The table's own footnote — reports "read as a one-day move" — is the tell. A month/day swap
moves a date by weeks or months. These reports move it by exactly one day. That is not this bug.

## The three problems

1. **The parser bug.** The shared parser reads `MM/DD` no matter the tenant's locale, and every
   tenant here writes day-first. **374 reports.** → [issue #1](https://github.com/aealvarengal/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1)
2. **The hint bug.** `booking-v3` shows a `MM/DD/YYYY` hint to day-first-locale tenants, so people
   type month-first on purpose, and the form reads it the tenant's way anyway. Same symptom,
   different cause, only on `form` submissions from `booking-v3`. **33 reports.** →
   [issue #2](https://github.com/aealvarengal/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2)
3. **The one-day bug.** Five reports where a date is off by exactly one day — a meeting that
   showed one date in Lisbon and another in Toronto, a month where every booking ran a day late
   and only that month, an invite that arrived before the booking it was for. Not a swap. A
   timezone or day-boundary problem, already flagged once in `desk-notes.json` and never split
   out. **5 reports.** → [issue #3](https://github.com/aealvarengal/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/3)

374 + 33 + 5 = 412.

## Why the desk didn't see it

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) is right that merging on subject is fast
and that splitting later is cheaper than two engineers chasing the same bug. It says nothing
about the other direction: two different bugs can also *share* a subject line, and merging them
hides that there were ever two. "Merge when unsure" needs a partner rule: **before you mark a
big ticket known, check whether every report in it actually shares a cause, not just a
subject.** `causes.ts` already has the logic to check this; #4471 just never ran it before
someone wrote the word "known" on the page.

## Lessons

- A ticket this size is not "mostly one bug reported many times" by default — it is one bug
  only if something actually checked.
- A footnote on a table is a finding somebody has to read and act on, not decoration. This one
  sat there since the table was built.
- The desk's own tool for telling causes apart (`causes.ts`) already existed. Nobody pointed it
  at #4471 until now.

